## 3. Per-file verdict for the resume-only content

### 3.1 `sealed-variants.test.ts` — WARRANTED (genuine fix)
Test line 245 (coordination tree), inside `sealed variant sample trees carry the ruled
declaration and printed forms` (test reads `reference/rust/gen/boring/sealed_variant_ops.rs` at
line 220):
- old: `expect(rust).toContain('return "NoneDrawKind".to_string();');`
- gen (`sealed_variant_ops.rs:35-36`, p09 snapshot + both worktrees byte-identical):
  `pub fn to_string(&self) -> UString { return UString::from("NoneDrawKind").to_ustring(); }`
- The old expectation is contradicted by the generator; the resume replacement matches it
  byte-for-byte (the `.to_ustring()` call is what the generator really emits — the patch pins
  reality).
- It was failing before: present in the completed agent's before-48 census; reproduced in my
  local before run; flipped to passing after (resume targeted-1→targeted-4; my local before→after).
- Root cause of the completed agent's miss: §3.14 of their REPORT claims the file "contains no
  Rust-target String/&str assertion" — a token scan over `String`/`&str` cannot see a
  string-literal call site in `"literal".to_string()` form. The independent
  `string-expectation-scope` census (line 302) had counted this line (`sealed-variants 1`) in its
  47-line table. So the completed agent's "45/45 string-family lines fixed" is actually
  45/46; the 46th is exactly this line.
- The file's other 3 tests are fresh-generation spawn tests that time out environmentally in every
  run (before and after, both worktrees) — not patch-relevant.

### 3.2 `enum-queries.test.ts`, `record-copy.test.ts` — the brief's premise is stale
Both files are in BOTH patches with byte-identical hunks (verified programmatically). Both fixes
are genuine (`float_width.rs:42`, `record_ops.rs:19/23/27`). There is nothing resume-specific to
adjudicate; they were presumably "extra" in an earlier iteration of the resume patch, before it
converged on the common 12-file core.

### 3.3 The one true behavioral conflict: `static-state.test.ts` final hunk
- Generator fact (`static_state_ops.rs:29`):
  `pub const STATIC_STATE_OPS_EMPTY_MARK: &UStr = unsafe { &*(&[0x0065u16, 0x006Du16, 0x0070u16, 0x0074u16, 0x0079u16] as *const [u16] as *const crate::runtime::u_string::UStr) };`
  — the current generator emits a double pointer cast in the `&UStr` constant lane.
- Completed patch: keeps `not.toContain(" as ")` → test `static fields generated trees > Rust
  uses Mutex guards and the direct constant lane` FAILS (one of their 35 residual names). Their
  stance: the guard is a cast-form guard, not a string spelling; loosening = forbidden weakening.
- Resume patch: strips exactly the `as *const [u16] as *const <path>` lane, then keeps the no-cast
  assertion on the remainder → test PASSES (resume targeted-3 line 8; my local after run).
  The regex is precise (strips only that lane; every other ` as ` occurrence stays covered).
- Assessment: **supported by generator output, but it weakens a test assertion** — the hard rule
  "do not weaken any test" cuts against silently carrying it. The correct disposition is a
  deliberate sign-off: either formally update the guard (as the resume patch does, with a comment)
  or keep it red as a tracked residual until the generator's EMPTY_MARK lane is re-decided. My
  verification run used the resume patch as-is, so the flip is measured, not assumed.

